### Example Patent Claims

1. **A lighting system comprising:**  
   a plurality of luminaires, each luminaire comprising at least one light source and a communication interface, and each luminaire being configured to provide luminaire-identification data indicative of a luminaire type; and  
   a controller communicatively coupled to the plurality of luminaires, the controller being configured to:  
   determine, for each luminaire, a position and an orientation of the luminaire within an environment;  
   obtain the luminaire-identification data for each luminaire;  
   determine respective operating parameters for the plurality of luminaires based on the position, the orientation, and the luminaire type of each luminaire and a target lighting scene;  
   control the plurality of luminaires according to the respective operating parameters to produce the target lighting scene;  
   detect a change in at least one of the position or the orientation of at least one luminaire; and  
   in response to detecting the change, update at least one of the respective operating parameters to maintain the target lighting scene.

2. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein the luminaire type identifies at least one of a light-output distribution, a beam angle, an output intensity range, a color range, a spectral characteristic, or an aiming capability of the corresponding luminaire.

3. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein each luminaire further comprises at least one sensor configured to generate localization data usable to determine the position or the orientation of the luminaire.

4. The lighting system of claim 3, wherein the at least one sensor comprises one or more of an inertial measurement unit, an accelerometer, a gyroscope, a magnetometer, an image sensor, a depth sensor, a radio-frequency receiver, or an optical receiver.

5. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to determine the position or the orientation of each luminaire based on signals exchanged between the luminaire and one or more reference devices having known locations.

6. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein the target lighting scene specifies a desired spatial distribution of at least one of illuminance, luminance, color, correlated color temperature, or spectral power within the environment.

7. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein updating the at least one operating parameter comprises adjusting one or more of an intensity, a color, a spectrum, a beam shape, a beam direction, or an on-off state of at least one luminaire.

8. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein the controller is further configured to compensate for movement of a first luminaire by modifying an operating parameter of a second luminaire.

9. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein each luminaire is configured to transmit the luminaire-identification data automatically upon connection to the lighting system.

10. The lighting system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to determine the respective operating parameters by applying an optical model associated with the luminaire type to the determined position and orientation of each luminaire.

11. **A computer-implemented method for controlling a plurality of luminaires, the method comprising:**  
    receiving, from or for each luminaire, luminaire-identification data identifying a luminaire type;  
    determining a position and an orientation of each luminaire within an environment;  
    obtaining scene data defining a target lighting scene;  
    calculating respective operating parameters for the plurality of luminaires based on the scene data and the position, the orientation, and the luminaire type of each luminaire;  
    operating the plurality of luminaires according to the respective operating parameters;  
    determining that at least one luminaire has been moved; and  
    responsive to determining that the at least one luminaire has been moved, recalculating at least a portion of the respective operating parameters such that the plurality of luminaires continues to produce the target lighting scene.

12. The method of claim 11, wherein determining that the at least one luminaire has been moved comprises detecting a change exceeding a threshold in the position or the orientation of the at least one luminaire.

13. The method of claim 11, further comprising measuring an actual lighting condition within the environment and recalculating the respective operating parameters based additionally on a difference between the actual lighting condition and the target lighting scene.

14. The method of claim 11, wherein recalculating the at least a portion of the respective operating parameters comprises redistributing a requested light output among two or more of the plurality of luminaires.

15. **A luminaire comprising:**  
    at least one light source;  
    a memory storing luminaire-identification data indicative of an optical output characteristic of the luminaire;  
    a localization subsystem configured to generate data indicative of a position and an orientation of the luminaire;  
    a communication interface configured to provide the luminaire-identification data and the data indicative of the position and the orientation to a lighting controller; and  
    control circuitry configured to receive, from the lighting controller, an operating command determined based on the luminaire-identification data, the position, the orientation, and a target lighting scene, and to operate the at least one light source according to the operating command.

16. The luminaire of claim 15, wherein the localization subsystem is configured to detect movement of the luminaire and cause updated data indicative of the position or the orientation to be transmitted to the lighting controller.

17. The luminaire of claim 15, wherein the optical output characteristic comprises at least one of a photometric distribution, a maximum luminous flux, a beam angle, a spectral range, or an available set of color-control parameters.
